WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.895
Upload features consume real resources:
bandwidth, memory, CPU, disk,

2
00:00:03.975 --> 00:00:06.591
object storage, database capacity, scanner
capacity,

3
00:00:06.671 --> 00:00:10.010
queue capacity, application-server
threads, and support attention.

4
00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.317
Resource abuse can be accidental or
malicious.

5
00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:14.790
A normal user may upload the wrong file
repeatedly,

6
00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.098
while an attacker may try to exhaust
storage or processing capacity.

7
00:00:18.178 --> 00:00:22.074
The controls should protect the platform
while preserving legitimate workflows.

8
00:00:22.154 --> 00:00:25.772
Apply limits based on user, tenant, API
key, IP address, endpoint, file type,

9
00:00:25.852 --> 00:00:26.965
and business workflow.

10
00:00:27.045 --> 00:00:30.718
Useful controls include quotas, rate
limits, size limits, file count limits,

11
00:00:30.798 --> 00:00:33.692
timeout controls, concurrency limits,
queue backpressure,

12
00:00:33.772 --> 00:00:36.388
scanner capacity limits, and storage
lifecycle rules.

13
00:00:36.468 --> 00:00:38.305
The right limits depend on the use case.

14
00:00:38.385 --> 00:00:40.889
A customer document portal, profile image
endpoint,

15
00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:44.977
and administrative import feature should
not inherit the same upload budget without

16
00:00:45.057 --> 00:00:45.502
thought.

17
00:00:45.582 --> 00:00:49.199
Plan for failed uploads, interrupted
uploads, duplicate uploads, retries,

18
00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:50.782
and abandoned temporary files.

19
00:00:50.862 --> 00:00:54.981
Cleanup jobs should remove orphaned
temporary content and expired quarantined files.

20
00:00:55.061 --> 00:00:58.233
Expensive processing such as image
resizing, archive extraction,

21
00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:02.209
document conversion, OCR, and malware
scanning should be bounded and observable.

22
00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:06.018
A feature that accepts files faster than
it can scan or process them can become an

23
00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:08.770
operational backlog and a security
visibility problem.

24
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.189
Monitoring should cover unusual upload
volume, repeated rejections,

25
00:01:12.269 --> 00:01:15.441
scanner failures, storage growth, queue
age, processing latency,

26
00:01:15.521 --> 00:01:18.805
access-denied patterns, cleanup failures,
and download anomalies.

27
00:01:18.885 --> 00:01:21.000
Alerts should be actionable, not just
noisy.

28
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.253
A good alert helps defenders understand
whether the issue is abuse,

29
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:28.062
a broken client, a configuration mistake,
scanner failure, storage pressure,

30
00:01:28.142 --> 00:01:29.700
or a downstream service problem.
